    "alert_id": "ALERT_TDF_RENAL_TREND",
    "title": "Chronic Kidney Disease Risk on TDF",
    "message": "Guidelines highlight the importance of monitoring renal function in patients on TDF, especially during intercurrent illness. This patient's creatinine has risen steadily from 0.9 mg/dL in 2020 to 1.4 mg/dL in 2026, with a current eGFR of 52 mL/min/1.73m2 indicating Stage 3a CKD. Urinalysis shows protein 1+. TDF is a known nephrotoxin. However, the patient also takes ibuprofen 2-3 times weekly, which can independently cause renal impairment. The chronic, progressive trend is concerning regardless of attribution.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Assess TDF and NSAID use. Consider switching TDF to a non-tenofovir alafenamide (TAF) or abacavir-based regimen per guidelines if renal impairment is confirmed.",
    "citations": [
      {"page_number": 40, "chunk_id": "sahcs_adult_art_2024__sahcs_adult_art_2024__p40_c2"}
    ]
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "ALERT_MONITORING_GAP_PROTEINURIA",
    "title": "Proteinuria Needs Evaluation",
    "message": "The patient's urinalysis shows protein 1+ and a trace of glucose. Proteinuria is a marker of kidney damage and, alongside the declining eGFR, suggests possible chronic kidney disease. The guideline excerpt links uncontrolled hypertension and diabetes to complications like chronic kidney disease. While the patient has no known hypertension or diabetes, the proteinuria requires investigation to rule out these or other underlying conditions.",
    "issue_type": "monitoring_gap",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "Investigate cause of proteinuria: check blood pressure, fasting blood glucose, and consider urine protein-to-creatinine ratio.",
    "citations": [
      {"page_number": 43, "chunk_id": "hiv_guidelines_co_infectionscreening.extraction__hiv_guidelines_co_infectionscreening.extraction__p43_c2"}
    ]
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "ALERT_NSAID_RENAL_RISK",
    "title": "NSAID Use with Renal Impairment",
    "message": "The patient reports taking ibuprofen 400mg 2-3 times per week for headaches. NSAIDs like ibuprofen are nephrotoxic and can worsen pre-existing renal impairment. This is a compounding risk factor given the patient's documented chronic kidney disease (eGFR 52 mL/min/1.73m2) and proteinuria. Concurrent use with TDF, another nephrotoxin, significantly increases the risk of accelerated kidney injury.",
    "issue_type": "drug_interaction",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Strongly advise discontinuation of ibuprofen. Recommend alternative analgesics (e.g., paracetamol) for headache management.",
    "citations": []
